added I009, I010, I011, I012, I013, I014; dropped none. No run has met the target yet. The best so far is E006: 128/384/576 at 9.5 epochs, 0.7475 accuracy in 8.76 s. E004 showed that widening the last group is nearly free per step and buys sample efficiency. So the top idea runs 128/384/768 at 9.25 epochs, expected at about 0.755 in about 8.9 s, which should make it the first feasible incumbent. A second idea pushes the last group to 1024 at 8.5 epochs to find where extra width stops paying. Two exploit ideas go after per-step cost in the high-resolution group 1, which E004 identified as the expensive part: one cuts it from 3 convs to 2, the other narrows it from 128 to 96 channels. Each is judged against the ~1 pp per second exchange rate measured in E002. The explore ideas are a decisive label-smoothing 0.2 vs 0.3 test (H1.3.1) and loss-based data filtering (H1.6.1). Queued I007 (Muon) and I008 (progressive resizing) stay, because their hypotheses are still open. I dropped nothing. I left out the random-flip ablation (H1.2.1) and Cutout (H1.3.2) for now: both are expected to lower accuracy and would add information only. The queue now has 8 queued ideas.
